  - [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m — [38;5;153m(user_id, task_id)[39m primary key, plus [38;5;153mstate[39m ([38;5;153mwatching[39m | [38;5;153mmuted[39m) and [38;5;153mcreated_at[39m.
  - [38;5;153mtask_events[39m — [38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153mtask_id[39m, [38;5;153mactor_id[39m, [38;5;153mtype[39m ([38;5;153mstatus_changed[39m | [38;5;153massigned[39m | [38;5;153munassigned[39m | [38;5;153mdue_date_changed[39m | [38;5;153medited[39m),
    [38;5;153mold_value[39m, [38;5;153mnew_value[39m, [38;5;153mcreated_at[39m. Append-only; nothing updates or deletes a row here.
  - [38;5;153mnotifications[39m — [38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153muser_id[39m, [38;5;153mevent_id[39m, [38;5;153mread_at[39m (nullable), [38;5;153mcreated_at[39m, with a unique constraint on [38;5;153m(user_id, [39m
    [38;5;153mevent_id)[39m so a retried fan-out can't double-notify.

  One detail worth your attention, because it's the kind of thing that's annoying to change later: [38;5;153msubscriptions.state[39m
  exists instead of just deleting the row on unwatch. If unwatch deleted the row, the auto-subscribe rule would silently
  re-add you the next time someone assigned you that task — you'd have muted something and it would come back. A [38;5;153mmuted[39m
  row is a tombstone that auto-subscribe checks and respects.

  [1mModules[0m, each with one job and a narrow interface:

  - [38;5;153mdb[39m — schema, migrations, connection. Everything else goes through it.
  - [38;5;153mtasks[39m — task reads and mutations. Notably does [3mnot[0m know notifications exist.
  - [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m — [38;5;153mwatch[39m, [38;5;153munwatch[39m, [38;5;153mautoSubscribe[39m, and [38;5;153mresolveSubscribers(taskId)[39m. Sole owner of the question "who cares
    about this task."
  - [38;5;153mevents[39m — [38;5;153mrecord(taskId, actorId, type, old, new)[39m. Append-only writer.
  - [38;5;153mnotifications[39m — [38;5;153mfanOut(event)[39m, [38;5;153mlistFor(userId)[39m, [38;5;153munreadCount(userId)[39m, [38;5;153mmarkRead[39m, [38;5;153mmarkAllRead[39m.
  - [38;5;153mweb[39m — HTTP routes and HTML templates. No business logic.

  The seam that matters: [38;5;153mtasks[39m never calls [38;5;153mnotifications[39m. A thin service function wraps each mutation — apply the
  change, record the event, fan out — all inside one SQLite transaction, so you cannot get a task change without its
  notifications or vice versa. The "don't notify the actor" rule lives in exactly one place, inside [38;5;153mfanOut[39m, rather than
  being re-remembered at every call site. That's the specific failure mode approach C had, and it's worth spending one
  indirection to avoid.

  Does this look right so far?

